  "text": "perspectives. for instance, if a feature has a 90% approval rate, it's\ncrucial to analyze the reasons behind the 10% disapproval as well. this\nhelps in identifying potential improvements that could enhance user\nsatisfaction.\nseeking diverse opinions is another effective strategy. by involving\ncolleagues from various departments—such as marketing, engineering,\nand customer support—you can gather a wider range of insights. this\ndiversity can uncover blind spots in your understanding and lead to more\nbalanced interpretations of data.\nadditionally, employing neutral language when soliciting feedback is\nvital. instead of leading questions that may skew responses, opt for open-\nended inquiries that encourage honest opinions. for example, instead of\nasking if users \"love\" a feature, ask how they \"feel\" about it. this subtle\nshift can yield more genuine insights.\nlastly, always strive to gather more context. recognizing when you lack\nsufficient data is crucial; actively seeking additional information can lead\nto more informed and effective design decisions. by implementing these\nstrategies, designers can reduce the risk of framing bias and create more\nuser-centered products.\nwas this helpful? (7 yes £4) no\n\nzx enhance ~ ® al tutor preferences\n\nzx enhance v\n\n@ simplify\n",
